  ],
  "text": "Two thirds of an octopus's neurons are not in its head. They are in its arms. Your arm waits to be told what to do. Every single time. An octopus arm does not wait. It works the problem out by itself. There is a central brain, and then eight more clusters. One in every arm. Its blood runs on copper instead of iron, which is why it is blue. Three hearts. And skin cells that can sense light with no eyes involved. Our last shared ancestor was a simple worm, five hundred million years ago. So this intelligence was built from scratch. None of it is a copy of ours. It is the closest thing to an alien mind that we can actually keep in a tank. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
